INT. STARLIGHT ROLLER RINK - NIGHT

A mirror ball turns slowly over a dead floor. Dust lies on the skate rental counter. Somewhere an old organ wheezes a note and stops.

CORNELIUS "NEIL" PRATT, fifties, in a rumpled suit, carries a lantern and a ledger. MILDRED ASHCROFT, sixties, his realtor, follows in a tweed coat with her keys held like a weapon. FITZ, a lean teenager with a flashlight and a tool bag, brings up the rear.

                    NEIL
          Mildred, you said the power was off.

                    MILDRED
          It is, Neil.

                    NEIL
          Then who is playing the organ?

                    MILDRED
          The building settles. It makes noises.

Fitz crouches by the wall and pokes at a fuse box. He whistles through his teeth, a tuneless little line.

                    FITZ
          Mr. Pratt, the box is cold. Dead for years.

All three turn. At the far end of the rink, a pair of skates, white leather, laces tied, sits at the edge of the floor, as if someone has just stepped out of them.

Mildred takes a step back. Neil steps forward and lifts his lantern. Fitz swings his flashlight at the skates and holds it there, the beam very steady.

                    NEIL
          Those weren't there when we came in.

                    MILDRED
          Nonsense. They were.

                    FITZ
          Mildred, they weren't.

Nobody moves. The mirror ball turns. Spots of light cross their faces, one after another.

The skates slide a few inches toward them across the floor, wheels humming. Neil, Mildred, and Fitz stop breathing together. The wheels go quiet.

                    MILDRED
          (carefully, to nobody)
          Well. We'll note it in the listing.

She writes in her notebook, her pen perfectly smooth. Neil sets down the lantern.

                    NEIL
          Fitz, go and check the doors.

                    FITZ
          They're locked, sir. From outside.

The organ plays one low chord. The three of them stay where they are, watching the skates, which do not move again.
